1. Make D the new parent of G.
2. Make N the parent of D.
3. Update P to be the parent of N, if it exists.
4. If P is not null, check if P's balance factor has changed and rebalance as needed.

Now let's consider a single right rotation (RR), which is the mirror image of LL. We have a node N with two children, G (the grandchild) and D (the child), where N is the left child of its parent P. The goal is to move N up as the new parent of D and make G the child of N.

[KB-Image "AVL_RR_rotation"]

The steps for an RR rotation are:
1. Make D the new parent of G.
2. Make N the parent of D.
3. Update P to be the parent of N, if it exists.
4. If P is not null, check if P's balance factor has changed and rebalance as needed.

The LR and RL rotations are combinations of a left rotation followed by a right rotation (LR) or vice versa (RL). These double rotations help maintain the height balance in more complex scenarios.

For more information about AVL trees, you can refer to [KB-Text "Introduction to Algorithms" by Cormen et al.] and [GK] various online resources on data structures and algorithms.
