   - This happens when a node's balance factor is 2 on the right side, and its left child has a balance factor of -1 (i.e., the left child is heavy).
   - To rebalance the tree, we first perform a left rotation on the left child (LL rotation) and then perform a right rotation on the parent node around the new root of the left subtree. This results in an LR rotation on the original node.

[KB-Image "AVL_LR_Rotation"]

3. Right-Right (RR) Rotation:
   - Similar to LL, this occurs when the right child of a node is heavy (i.e., its balance factor is 2).
   - To balance the tree, we perform an RR rotation, which means rotating the right subtree clockwise around the current node.

[KB-Image "AVL_RR_Rotation"]

4. Right-Left (RL) Rotation:
   - This happens when a node's balance factor is 2 on the left side, and its right child has a balance factor of 1 (i.e., the right child is heavy).
   - To rebalance the tree, we first perform a right rotation on the right child (RR rotation) and then perform a left rotation on the parent node around the new root of the right subtree. This results in an RL rotation on the original node.

[KB-Image "AVL_RL_Rotation"]

These rotations help maintain the height balance in AVL trees, ensuring efficient performance for various operations. For more information about AVL trees and their applications, you can refer to [KB-Text "Introduction to Algorithms" by Cormen et al.] or search online resources related to data structures and algorithms.

