   - Left Rotation (LL and LR cases): In a left-left case, we perform a right rotation on the root of the left subtree (the node with BF = 2). This is also known as a single left rotation. Similarly, in a left-right case, we first perform a left rotation on the left child of the root and then a right rotation on the original root. This is called a double left rotation.
   [KB-Image "AVL_Left_Rotation"]

   - Right Rotation (RR and RL cases): In a right-right case, we perform a left rotation on the root of the right subtree (the node with BF = 2). This is also known as a single right rotation. Similarly, in a right-left case, we first perform a right rotation on the right child of the root and then a left rotation on the original root. This is called a double right rotation.
   [KB-Image "AVL_Right_Rotation"]

2. Double Rotation (LLRR and RRLL): In some cases, two rotations are needed to balance the tree. For example, in an LLRR case, we first perform a left rotation on the left child of the root (LL) and then a right rotation on the new root (RR). Similarly, in an RRLL case, we first perform a right rotation on the right child of the root (RR) and then a left rotation on the new root (LL).
   [KB-Image "AVL_Double_Rotation"]

These rotations help maintain the balance factor within the specified range (-1 ≤ BF ≤ 1), ensuring the AVL tree remains approximately balanced. For more detailed explanations and examples, you can refer to [KB-Text "Data Structures and Algorithms in Modern Computer Science"].

[GK] The concept of AVL trees was introduced by Georgy Adelson-Marsál and Aleksej Adelsón-Velskij in 1962 as a solution for maintaining the balance in binary search trees. It is widely used in applications requiring efficient insertion, deletion, and search operations on large data sets.
