    /   \
   B     F
  /     / \
 A   D   E
```

Now the height of the left and right subtrees of 'I' is balanced. This is an example of a right rotation (R).

Similarly, if we have an overbalanced right subtree at node 'J', we perform a left rotation on 'J':

```
       I
     /   \
    B     J
  /     / \
 A   D   E
      / \
     F   B
```

Now, the height of the left and right subtrees of 'J' is balanced. This is an example of a left rotation (L).

The specific type of rotation to be performed depends on the balance factor of each node. The balance factor of a node is calculated as the height of the right subtree minus the height of the left subtree, and adjustments are made after insertions or deletions to maintain the balance [KB-Text "Introduction to Algorithms" by Cormen et al.].

In summary, AVL tree rotation works by performing specific rotations (LL, LR, RL, RR) on nodes to balance overbalanced subtrees and ensure that the height of the left and right child subtrees is always within one. This helps in maintaining the logarithmic time complexity for search operations.
