1. **Single Rotation - Left Rotation [KB-Text "Introduction to Algorithms"]**
   ![AVL Tree Single Left Rotation](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Avl_single_left_rotation.svg/250px-Avl_single_left_rotation.svg.png "[KB-Image 'AVL Tree Single Left Rotation']")

   In the case of left-heavy tree, we perform a left rotation on the root node (Z). The right child of the new root becomes the left child of the old root, and the old root becomes the right child of the original right child.

2. **Single Rotation - Right Rotation [KB-Text "Introduction to Algorithms"]**
   ![AVL Tree Single Right Rotation](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Avl_single_right_rotation.svg/250px-Avl_single_right_rotation.svg.png "[KB-Image 'AVL Tree Single Right Rotation']")

   In the case of right-heavy tree, we perform a right rotation on the root node (Z). The left child of the new root becomes the right child of the old root, and the old root becomes the left child of the original left child.

3. **Double Rotation [KB-Text "Introduction to Algorithms"]**
   Double rotations are a combination of two single rotations. They can be either left-left (LL) or right-right (RR) or left-right (LR) and right-left (RL). The goal is to balance the tree after an insertion or deletion operation that causes the height difference between subtrees to exceed 1.

   - **Left-Left (LL) Double Rotation**
     ![AVL Tree LL Double Rotation](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Avl_ll_double_rotation.svg/250px-Avl_ll_double_rotation.svg.png "[KB-Image 'AVL Tree LL Double Rotation']")

   - **Right-Right (RR) Double Rotation**
     ![AVL Tree RR Double Rotation](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Avl_rr_double_rotation.svg/250px-Avl_rr_double_rotation.svg.png "[KB-Image 'AVL Tree RR Double Rotation']")

   - **Left-Right (LR) and Right-Left (RL) Double Rotations**
     These double rotations are a combination of one left rotation followed by a right rotation or vice versa, depending on the case. They help balance the tree when the height difference between subtrees is more than 2 after an insertion or deletion operation.

In summary, AVL tree rotation works by performing single rotations (left and right) and double rotations (LL, RR, LR, and RL) to maintain the balance of the tree, ensuring that the height difference between subtrees is at most 1. This allows for efficient search, insertion, and deletion operations in self-balancing binary search trees.

[GK] AVL trees were invented by Geodesic numbers theorist Adelson-Velsky and Soviet computer scientist Landis in 1972 as an improvement over the earlier B-trees. They are widely used in applications that require fast insertion, deletion, search, and minimum or maximum operations on large data sets.
