2025-04-02 21:43:15,238 - INFO -  The AVL (Adelson-Velsky and Landis) tree is a self-balancing binary search tree, which means it maintains the height of the tree close to logarithmic time complexity. This is achieved by performing rotations on nodes that violate certain balance conditions.

There are two types of rotations in AVL trees: left rotation (LL and LR) and right rotation (RR and RL). Let's consider a node N as the root, where N has a subtree T1 on its left side and a subtree T2 on its right side.

1. Left-Left (LL) Rotation:
   - If N is the root of the tree, then perform a right rotation on N with T1 as the new root. [GK] This operation is performed when T1's height increases by 2 while T2's height remains unchanged or increases by 1.
   - If N is not the root and T1's parent (P) is on the left side of P, then perform a right rotation on P with N as the new left child, followed by a left rotation on N with T1 as the new right child. [GK] This operation is performed when both T1 and T2 increase in height by 1.

   Example:
   ```
   Before Rotation:
       A
     /   \
    B     C
   / \     |
  D   E    F

   After LL Rotation:
        B
      /   \
     A     C
   / \     |
  D   E    F
   ```

2. Left-Right (LR) Rotation:
   - If N is the root of the tree, then perform a left rotation on N with T2 as the new root. [GK] This operation is performed when T1's height remains unchanged or increases by 1 while T2's height increases by 2.
   - If N is not the root and T1's parent (P) is on the right side of P, then perform a left rotation on P with T2 as the new right child, followed by a right rotation on N with T1 as the new left child. [GK] This operation is performed when both T1 and T2 increase in height by 1.

   Example:
   ```
   Before Rotation:
        A
       /
      B
     / | \
    C D   E
          |
          F

   After LR Rotation:
           B
         /   \
        A     E
       / |     |
      C D    F   B
   ```

3. Right-Right (RR) and Right-Left (RL) rotations can be handled similarly by swapping left and right in the descriptions above. [KB-Text "Introduction to Algorithms, 3rd edition, CLRS"]
