2025-04-02 21:43:15,238 - INFO -  AVL (Adelson-Velsky and Landis) trees are self-balancing binary search trees that maintain the height of the tree close to logarithmic in the number of nodes, ensuring efficient insertion, deletion, and search operations. The balancing is achieved using rotations, specifically left rotation (LL, LR, RL, andRR), which are performed to maintain the AVL property: the difference between heights of two child subtrees for any node should not exceed 1.

Let's take a look at each type of rotation:

1. **Left Rotation (LL and LR)**
   - LL rotation is performed on a left-leaning tree where both children of the root are leaning to the left. [KB-Image "AVL_LL"]
     ```
     A           B
    / \         /
   B  C     =>  A
                \
                 D
     ```
   - LR rotation is performed on a tree where the left child of the root is leaning to the left and the left child of the left child is leaning to the right. [KB-Image "AVL_LR"]
     ```
     A           B
    / \         / \
   B  D        C   A
      |            |
      E           B
     ```

2. **Right Rotation (RL and RR)**
   - RL rotation is performed on a right-leaning tree where both children of the root are leaning to the right. [KB-Image "AVL_RL"]
     ```
     A           B
      \         /
       C        A
        |       |
        D       E
     ```
   - RR rotation is performed on a tree where the right child of the root is leaning to the right and the right child of the right child is leaning to the left. [KB-Image "AVL_RR"]
     ```
     A           B
      \         /
       C        A
       |       |
       D       E
      / \     /
     F   G    B
     ```

These rotations are used to maintain the balance in the tree, ensuring efficient operations. For more details, you can refer to [KB-Text "AVL Tree"] for a comprehensive explanation of AVL trees and their properties.

[GK] The concept of AVL trees was introduced by Georgy Adelson-Marsál and Aleksej Landis in 1962 as an improvement over the earlier proposed B-trees, which were developed by Rudolf Bayer and Edward McCreight in 1972.
